7,544],"attention_weights":[0.7519,0.0254,0.0189],"max_activation":7.562329292297363,"max_token_index":550,"peak_labels":{"hate speech":"none","language":"en","names":"none","part of speech":"ADV","personal data":"none","postal addresses":"none","source":"ultrachat","toxicity":"none"},"text":"<|im_start|>system\nYou are a helpful assistant.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nRead the passage below and answer the question or follow the instruction: Who is Elliot Perry and what is his background?\n\nPassage: But the Memphis Grizzlies should win the NBA lottery because they are sending a young man named Courtney Love to represent the franchise in Chicago.\nThe Grizzlies could win the NBA lottery because of Elliot Perry’s lucky socks.\nThe Grizzlies could win the NBA lottery because they have the second-best odds.\nBut the Grizzlies really should win the NBA lottery because they are sending Perry and a young man named Courtney Love to represent the franchise in Chicago, which is what the organization is all about.\nSo you can have your four-leaf clovers, your lucky charms, your horseshoes, your lucky pennies and your rabbit’s feet.\nThe Grizzlies are going with an eighth-grader who was reading at three years below grade level when he started at Grizzlies Prep but is now reading at four years above grade level, and who takes care of his mother who is legally blind.\nTo be clear: The NBA has a rule that the actual representative on stage has to be connected to the franchise. So Perry, one of the team's minority owners, will be the one stage.\nThat’s just right, too, of course. Perry represents the best of this town. He’s the perfect example of what young men in Memphis can become if they have the right support.\nPerry’s grandfather, Jessie Perry, dug ditches for the city. He is one of the men captured in Ernest Withers’ famous “I am a man” photograph.\nPerry never thought about going to college until an older, neighborhood kid named Michael Toney took him to the University of Memphis campus and showed him what it was all about.\nPerry had to take the ACT four times before he could get a qualifying score. He went on to star at Memphis, played more than a decade in the NBA, and then returned to the city where he grew up, so he could be to kids like Love what Toney had been to him.\nThat’s what’s different about the Grizzlies organization. The people who brought it to Memphis — and the people who run it today — understand that life is a lottery, too.\nDid you grow up with one parent or two? Did you grow up in this zip code or that? Did you go to a school where nearly everybody tests above grade level or where nearly everybody tests below?\nLove has been raised by his grandmother, Toyia Polk. His father lives in another state. His mother was diagnosed with glaucoma when she was 24, can only see light and dark out of her right eye and has extremely limited vision out of her left.\nLearning just wasn’t a big priority for Love before he arrived at Grizzlies Prep in sixth grade. It certainly wasn’t because he was dumb.\nThe mission of the Grizzlies and Grizzlies Prep — the only school in the country that bears the name of a professional sports team — is to reverse this devastating trend before boys get to high school. It is a public, all-boys charter school at which scholar","tokens":["<","|","im","_","start","|>","system","\n","You"," are"," a"," helpful"," assistant",".<","|","im","_","end","|>","\n","<","|","im","_","start","|>","user","\n","Read"," the"," passage"," below"," and"," answer"," the"," question"," or"," follow"," the"," instruction",":"," Who"," is"," Elliot"," Perry"," and"," what"," is"," his"," background","?","\n","\n","Pass","age",":"," But"," the"," Memphis"," Gri","zz","lies"," should"," win"," the"," NBA"," lottery"," because"," they"," are"," sending"," a"," young"," man"," named"," Court","ney"," Love"," to"," represent"," the"," franchise"," in"," Chicago",".","\n","The"," Gri","zz","lies"," could"," win"," the"," NBA"